was a mistake because any  impact in the system impacted all of our  customers so one of the things that  we're working on is splitting that up  into smaller fault domains to limit the  number of people that are mad at us at  any given time so the design for the new  system is actually already done and it's  in testing right now and the plan is to  move to it in the next few months so  this is what our next phase of the  architecture looks like we're  essentially moving more to a streaming  model one of the things that we did was  that we rewrote our collection tier we  had all this third-party and open source  tools that we were using and it was  becoming more complicated and more  difficult to manage so we wrote our own  collection tier which gave us the  ability to classify data as it was being  streamed in and that way we were able to  separate this data out as it feeds into  Kafka and then we could upload that data  already classify it up into cloud  storage that gave us a single ingestion  tier to worry about and we're able to  get consistent stats and performance  across the entire tier from there the  process is very similar all the data  that goes into cloud storage it is  already in those Avro files that I  talked about earlier but once the file  is created we generate a notification  that goes into pub/sub our  transformation tier is now running on  kubernetes engine and so it's just using  a pulse description to listen for those  notifications once it gets one it reads  the  from cloud storage the streams the data  into bigquery some of the advantages of  this new system we're getting much  faster date availability with bigquery  streaming than we were with the batch  loads in some cases data is now  available in seconds as opposed to  minutes because we've moved a lot of our  transform workload over to gke we're now  able to take advantage of things like  kubernetes pot auto scaling to grow and  shrink our compute resources over the  course of a day and this particular  model addresses the problem of the last  model which was the data getting stuck  behind that older data so how does that  work let me dive into that for just a  second so the way this works is we built  essentially two paths for these  notifications and the notifications only  use one path at a time so as the data is  streamed up and written into cloud  storage we generate those notifications  on either the a path or the B path if  one path starts getting behind or  there's a backlog or any sort of problem  just like a railroad switch we just  switch the notifications to the  secondary path and from there all the  recent data can be streamed into  bigquery and the backlog data can just  flow in as it was flowing in before but  this gives us the opportunity to keep  recent data recent and available for our  users so a few lessons learned from our  experience building the system the first  thing is keep it simple  there's a lot of complexity in the  system already there's a lot of options  out there a lot of tools and things to  play with and tinker with but we only  started with two cloud services when we  built the system cloud storage and  bigquery that was it and we didn't move  forward with any additional cloud  services and so we felt like we had  really understood those services and the  impact of those services was going to  have on the overall system  all of the additional complexity that  you add to the system means that your  failures are going to be more complex  it's more your team has to learn so it's  really important to keep it simple using  those fault domains to limit the impact  it's another good lesson for us  simplicity and how many things can go  wrong will limit the overall impact to  the system when something does and  evidently go wrong the second thing is  make sure you iterate your zine you've  seen a couple of different evolutions of  our architecture and what we're about to  implement and I'm sure there'll be  changes down the road as well you want  to plan for failures in your system it's  not going to be perfect from day one we  certainly learn that we want to use  those feathers to help iterator design  and make those incremental improvements  a lot of the quotas and limits and stuff  that we ran into a lot of those can be  adjusted so it's important to stay  aligned with your customer engineering  team to make sure that you kind of stay  ahead of those particular limits and  then consider your workload placement  over time it doesn't have to be the case  that all of your workload moves to the  cloud from day one we certainly didn't  we started with a hybrid model we're  continuing with a hybrid model we have a  lot of workload that runs on from  removing different pieces to the cloud  as it fits our particular use case and  you should feel free to do the same  thing that is a good model to work with  and lastly focus on value and what I  mean by this is really think more about  outcomes and what you're trying to get  to for your customers then the  engineering effort a lot of times we get  excited by the engineering and that's ok  there's a lot of fun toys to play with  here but it's important to really think  about the outcome of what you're trying  to get to when you're using something  like big courier is a managed service  it's important to realize that there's a  lot of things you don't have to worry  about you can immediately start having  that conversation with your data and  your users I will benefit and then of  course think about ways to cut the  problem in half to leverage your  partners and to make sure  you're able to continue to use them as  sort of an extension of your engineering  team as you focus on those outcomes so  that's really it we my team has  certainly learned a lot building the  system and hopefully some of these  experiences will be helpful for you if  you're considering something similar  [Music]
 

